.TH HOST "1" "2026-10-19" "Kendra Electronic Wonderworks" "Local Utilities"
.SH NAME
host - DNS lookup utility implementation for BSD 4.3
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BR host
.IB {name}
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR host
is a simple utility for performing DNS lookups on BSD 4.3. It is normally used to convert names to IP addresses and vice versa.

.IB name 
is the domain name that is to be looked up. It can also be a dotted-decimal IPv4 address, in which case 
.BR host
by default performs a reverse lookup for that address.

When no arguments are given, 
.BR host
prints a short summary of its command-line argument.
.SH OPTIONS
Unlike modern implementations of this utility, the 
.BR host 
implementation for BSD 4.3 does not have options.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gethostbyname (3)
.BR gethostbyaddr(3)
.BR resolver(5)
.SH BUGS
No known bugs.

The lack of modern bells and whistles could be considered a bug, but at the same time even without frills the program fulfills its basic function of address and name resolution.

Futhermore, year BSD 4.3 was released (1985), the Internet was a much simpler place. IPv6 didn't exist, nor did internationalized domain name, and numerous record types (like MX) didn't exist either.

(Nor, for that matter, did the Morris Worm.)

